# Releases the upgrade lane accepts as not-Ready after the upgrade, one
# `namespace/name` per line; everything after the reference is free text and
# should say what it is and where it is tracked.
#
# This file is read ONLY by the upgrade lane (hack/e2e-upgrade-apply.bats passes
# it to hack/e2e-wait-hr-ready.sh as EXPECTED_NOT_READY_FILE). The install gate
# never sees it, so nothing here can weaken the per-PR e2e suite.
#
# It exists because that lane is advisory: red for a known reason, every run,
# teaches reviewers to ignore it, and then it stops working on the day it finds
# something new. An entry buys exactly one thing — a failure that is already
# tracked stops hiding the next one.
#
# Entries expire on their own, and both rules are failures rather than warnings.
# A release listed here that is Ready again fails the lane until its line is
# deleted, and a line naming no release in the cluster fails it too. So the
# fastest way to remove a line is to fix the defect; the second fastest is to
# find out it was fixed already.
#
# Adding a line is a claim that the failure is understood well enough to be
# tracked elsewhere. If it is not, the lane going red is the correct outcome.

